-- title:
--   Theorem 1.2 (mathematical content) — the lattice-point count of an integral simplex is a signed sum over at most (2^d)^T primitive cones per vertex
-- statement:
--   Let $d\ge2$ and let $v_1,\dots,v_{k+1}\in\mathbb{Z}^d$ be affinely independent, so that $\Delta=\operatorname{conv}\{v_1,\dots,v_{k+1}\}$ is a $k$-dimensional integral simplex. For each vertex $v_j$ let $K_{v_j}$ be the supporting cone of $\Delta$ at $v_j$ (Definition 2.6), and let $\operatorname{Ind}_j$ be the index of the cone generated by the edge vectors $v_i-v_j$ ($i\ne j$). Then for each $j$ there are $N_j$ primitive cones $K_{j,i}=\operatorname{co}(g_{j,i})$, given by primitive generators $g_{j,i}$, and integers $\varepsilon_{j,i}$, $i=1,\dots,N_j$, such that
--
--   1. $N_j\le(2^d)^{T_j}$, where $T_j$ is the smallest integer with $T_j\ge(-\log\log1.9+\log\log\operatorname{Ind}_j)/(\log d-\log(d-1))$ ($T_j=0$ if $\operatorname{Ind}_j=1$);
--   2. $K_{v_j}=\sum_i\varepsilon_{j,i}K_{j,i}$, as characteristic functions on all of $\mathbb{R}^d$;
--   3. for every $c\in\mathbb{R}^d$ that is orthogonal to no generator of any $K_{j,i}$, each constant term $R(K_{j,i},v_j,c)$ of the Laurent expansion of
--   $$t\longmapsto\exp\{\langle t\cdot c,v_j\rangle\}\cdot\sigma(K_{j,i};t\cdot c)$$
--   at $t=0$ exists, and
--   $$\#(\Delta\cap\mathbb{Z}^d)=\sum_{j=1}^{k+1}\sum_{i=1}^{N_j}\varepsilon_{j,i}\,R(K_{j,i},v_j,c).$$
--
--   This is what Barvinok's polynomial-time algorithm computes: for fixed $d$ the number of terms is bounded by a polynomial in the input size, and each $R(K_{j,i},v_j,c)$ is an explicit rational function of inner products (Corollary 4.2).
--
--   **Formalization Note** Theorem 1.2 states that a polynomial time algorithm for Problem 1.1 exists. No machine model is formalized; the statement is the identity established in the proof of Theorem 1.2 (p. 778), with the size of the decompositions from the proof of Theorem 5.4 (p. 777). The decompositions are chosen first and the formula holds for every admissible $c$; the size bound depends only on $d$ and the indices. Added or fixed choices: $d\ge2$, because $T$ involves $\log(d-1)$; the generators of $K_{v_j}$ used for the index are the edge vectors $v_i-v_j$, as in the proof's "integral generators $u_1(v),\dots,u_k(v)$"; $c$ is real; $T=0$ when the index is $1$. The constant terms are unique when they exist, so the existential choice of the numbers $R$ is the paper's $R(K_i,v,c)$.
-- source:
--   Barvinok, A polynomial time algorithm for counting integral points in polyhedra when the dimension is fixed, Math. Oper. Res. 19 (1994), p. 769, Problem 1.1 and Theorem 1.2; proof on p. 778; count from the proof of Theorem 5.4, p. 777

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BarvinokCount_ShortFormula_sigma
import Definitions.Def_BarvinokCount_ShortFormula_integralSimplex
import Definitions.Def_BarvinokCount_ShortFormula_iterCount

namespace BarvinokCount.ShortFormula

theorem latticeCount_eq_short_signed_sum {d k : ℕ} (hd : 2 ≤ d)
    (v : Fin (k + 1) → Fin d → ℤ) (hv : AffineIndependent ℝ fun j => castVec (v j)) :
    ∃ (N : Fin (k + 1) → ℕ) (m : (j : Fin (k + 1)) → Fin (N j) → ℕ)
      (g : (j : Fin (k + 1)) → (i : Fin (N j)) → Fin (m j i) → Fin d → ℤ)
      (ε : (j : Fin (k + 1)) → Fin (N j) → ℤ),
      (∀ j i, IsPrimitiveGens (g j i)) ∧
      (∀ j, N j ≤ (2 ^ d) ^ iterCount d (coneIndex (edgeGens v j))) ∧
      (∀ j, IsSignedConeDecomp (supportingCone (simplex v) (castVec (v j))) (g j) (ε j)) ∧
      ∀ c : Fin d → ℝ, (∀ j i, IsRegular (g j i) c) →
        ∃ R : (j : Fin (k + 1)) → Fin (N j) → ℝ,
          (∀ j i, IsLaurentConstTerm
            (fun t => Real.exp (t * (c ⬝ᵥ castVec (v j))) * sigma (g j i) (t • c)) (R j i)) ∧
          (latticeCount (simplex v) : ℝ) = ∑ j, ∑ i, (ε j i : ℝ) * R j i := by sorry

end BarvinokCount.ShortFormula
